From 2fed336693e1ffcd1a6da5c0c62ba5d183f2368f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:57:47 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 116/119] feat: give the screen-space sun shafts an atmospheric
 condition (S8, approach A)

A crepuscular ray is single scattering along the view ray,
L = INTEGRAL sigma_s * p(theta) * E_perp * V dx, and CE's screen-space effect
implements none of the four factors that decide whether it exists. p(theta) is
approximated by the radial falloff, E_perp was restored by S2, V is a
brightness-and-depth heuristic - and sigma_s, the one that spans a factor of
50-1000 from clear air to fog, is simply absent. So the stock effect paints
rays on a bone-dry cloudless day at whatever RaysAmount the level authored.
A 100 m beam in clear air is about 4 % of the sky it is drawn against, which
is why nobody ever sees one.

On the scene-referred path only, the shaft composite is now scaled by

    cond = 1 - exp(-sigma_s * L_ref)

- sigma_s comes from the fog constants already published on the render thread
  every frame (SRenderViewShaderConstants): the volumetric fog height-density
  term when e_VolumetricFog is on, the legacy global fog term otherwise. Both
  are log2(e) * sigma at the viewer, both from the editor density through the
  same x 0.01, so no new constant buffer and no new plumbing. The VolFog2
  inscatter parameter doubles as the scattering albedo (default 1.0, a
  multiply by one at every stock preset).
- L_ref = r_ShaftAtmosphereRefLength, default 500 m. THE ONE INVENTED NUMBER,
  labelled as an assumption in the cvar help, the source and the docs, with
  the calibration procedure written and not yet run.
- One multiply on the composite constant, inside the branch ToneMapping.cpp
  already had for the exposed sun colour. The stock arm is not reached, so
  byte identity off the switch is structural rather than tested.

SunShafts_RaysAmount keeps its meaning and gains a better one: it is now the
MAXIMUM, reached in fog, which is where the picture wants it. The condition
only ever scales down. Nothing is disabled (D13).

r_ShaftAtmosphereCondition (default 1, 0 = off) is the escape hatch: a level
that authored zero fog and relied on the amount alone loses its shafts, which
is the intended correction, but "let it break where you can see it" is not
"break it with no way back".

r_HDRDebug 1 gains one line so the acceptance is read rather than judged by
eye - sigma_s, the visibility it implies, the condition, and the authored
amount beside the effective one. The round-3 lesson was that an unmeasured
shaft term is exactly how this went wrong the first time.

Docs: both cvars in ConsoleReference; the fog-density -> visibility table
(0.02 = 20 km, 0.1 = 3.9 km, 1.0 = 390 m) and the L_ref calibration procedure
in SceneReferredCalibration 3.1.

Known limits, on the record: the density is the one at the CAMERA, not along
the beam; local fog volumes never reach the global parameters; there is still
no occluder test. The hybrid with volumetric fog's froxel shafts (approach C)
has none of those problems and is the destination - this scalar is the part of
it that can be had for one multiply.

