 | Purpose | The frame time a capturing **sequence** forces; `t_FixedStep` is saved on start and restored on stop. Outside the editor nothing else locks frame time - `capture_frames` does not, whatever its name suggests - so without this a sequence is one file per rendered frame at whatever rate the machine managed, and N frames is not N/fps seconds. An EXR sequence is a timed artefact or it cannot be conformed. 0 = leave `t_FixedStep` alone and accept that the result has no cadence. |
 
+### r_ShaftAtmosphereCondition
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1 |
+| Purpose | Gives the screen-space sun shafts an **atmospheric condition** on the scene-referred path: the composite is scaled by `cond = 1 - exp(-sigma_s * L_ref)`, where `sigma_s` is the per-metre scattering coefficient of the fog system in force at the camera. 0 = off, the authored amount as-is. Ignored off the scene-referred path. |
+
+A crepuscular ray is single scattering, `L = INTEGRAL sigma_s * p(theta) * E_perp * V dx`. CE's
+screen-space effect carries no `sigma_s` at all, so it paints rays on a bone-dry cloudless day at
+whatever the level authored. Clear air to fog is a factor of 50-1000 in `sigma_s`, and nothing in
+the pass carried any of it — a 100 m beam in clear air is about **4 %** of the sky it is drawn
+against, which is why nobody ever sees one.
+
+`sigma_s` is taken from the fog constants already published on the render thread every frame
+(`SRenderViewShaderConstants`): the **volumetric fog** height-density term when `e_VolumetricFog`
+is on, the **legacy global fog** term otherwise. Both are `log2(e) * sigma` at the viewer's
+height, and both come from the editor's density through the same `x 0.01`, so the density slider
+*is* a visibility in metres. See `SceneReferredCalibration.md` §3 for the table.
+
+`SunShafts_RaysAmount` keeps its meaning and gains a better one: it is now the **maximum**, reached
+in fog. The condition only ever scales down.
+
+**The escape hatch, and why there is one.** A shipped level that authored zero fog and relied on
+the amount alone loses its shafts entirely at `1`. That is the intended correction, but "let it
+break where you can see it" is not "break it with no way back", and the lens-preset layer will
+eventually want a deliberate non-physical shaft. `0` is the honest way to say *this scene is not
+obeying the atmosphere*.
+
+Read the result with `r_HDRDebug 1`, which prints the line
+`shafts: sigma_s X /m (visibility ~Y km), condition Z (x), rays amount A -> effective B`.
+
+**Known limits, on the record:** the density is the one at the **camera**, not along the beam (a
+camera above a ground-fog layer reads as clear); local fog volumes never reach the global
+parameters, so they create no shafts; and there is still no occluder test — the mask remains a
+brightness-and-depth heuristic.
+
+### r_ShaftAtmosphereRefLength
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 500 (metres) |
+| Purpose | `L_ref` for `r_ShaftAtmosphereCondition`: the reference beam length the scattering coefficient is integrated over to turn a density into a 0..1 condition. Longer = shafts appear in thinner air; shorter = they need more fog. 0 suppresses the shafts entirely. |
+
+**This is a calibration constant, not a derived one — the one invented number in the feature.** It
+stands in for a path length a screen-space effect cannot know. The shaft mask is depth-weighted
+(`Sunshafts.cfx`), so its sources are structurally the far half of the frame, and 500 m is a
+defensible reference beam for an exterior scene. It is unmeasured; `SceneReferredCalibration.md`
+§3 says so and holds the procedure.
+
+Why it has no precedent to follow: screen-space shafts have no physical formulation anywhere to
+borrow a value from — UE, Frostbite and CE all treat the amount as an art parameter. The physics
+being approximated is standard; the mapping from it onto a screen-space radial blur is not, and
+cannot be.
+
